<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # In Ten Years, I Will... – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Time Capsule</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> In Ten Years, I Will...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of everyday conversations, with some repetition of particular words and phrases. Interactive: Respond appropriately when participating in discussions on familiar topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify and summarize key points from a conversation about career plans, including relevant advice (e.g., "The speaker plans to pursue software development and will take an internship with a local Panamanian tech company."). Can respond appropriately in a discussion about future goals and summarize peers' plans (e.g., "Carlos said that he is interested in studying marine biology."). Can comprehend and summarize ideas in a conversation about career advice (e.g., "The podcast suggested that you should reach out to local organizations to meet mentors in areas you are interested in for work.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing images of various future careers (e.g., software developer, engineer, doctor) and objects that could go into a time capsule (e.g., a letter, a photo, a small gadget). Students are asked to briefly share what they imagine their `future career` might be or what `goals` they would like to `achieve` in ten years. The teacher introduces the concept of a "time capsule" as a way to reflect on `future plans`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models 8-10 key vocabulary words relevant to `future plans` and `careers` from the curriculum list, such as: `career`, `future`, `goals`, `advice`, `internship`, `skills`, `opportunities`, `pursue`, `accomplish`, `recommend`, `high-demand`, `problem-solving`. Pronunciation is explicitly modeled, focusing on the long vowel /iː/ in words like "achieve" and "engineering", and stress in multisyllabic words like "leadership" and "problem-solving." The teacher uses gestures, visuals (flashcards), and short example sentences (e.g., "I will `pursue` a `career` in `engineering`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a `skill` is `high-demand`, does it mean many companies need it or only a few?" (Expected answer: Many companies need it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is an `internship` a paid job or a temporary training experience?" (Expected answer: A temporary training experience.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When you `pursue` a `goal`, are you trying to reach it or ignore it?" (Expected answer: Trying to reach it.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Time Capsule."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short interview (approx. 2-3 minutes) with a Panamanian high school student discussing their `future career plans`, `goals`, and `advice` they've received. The register is informal but clear, suitable for a B1.1 listener. The audio includes instances of `modals for possibilities` ("I `might pursue` `software development`"), `future continuous` ("I `will be working` as a `data analyst`"), and `reported speech` ("My teacher `said that I should` look for an `internship`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of everyday conversations, with some repetition of particular words and phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listen (Gist):</b> Students listen to the interview once and answer a general question: "What is the main topic the student is discussing?" (e.g., `future career plans` and `goals`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listen (Specific Details):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False activity based on statements related to the student's plans and `advice`. Example statements:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The student plans to `pursue` a `career` in `medicine`." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The student is considering an `internship` to gain `experience`." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The student's parents `recommended` developing `problem-solving skills`." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific vocabulary (e.g., `career`, `internship`, `skills`, `opportunities`, `advice`), grammar (Modals for career possibilities, Future Continuous, Reported Speech), and pronunciation (long /iː/, stress in multisyllabic words).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Cloze):</b> Students listen to short excerpts from the interview again. They have a worksheet with sentences from the audio, but 8-10 target vocabulary words (e.g., `career`, `goals`, `internship`, `advice`, `high-demand`, `opportunities`) are replaced with blanks. Students fill in the blanks with the correct words as they listen. E.g., "I want to `pursue` a ____ in `technology`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Grammar Identification & Pronunciation):</b> The teacher plays sentences containing modals (`will`, `might`, `could`) and future continuous from the audio. Students raise one finger if they hear a definite plan (e.g., `will be working`) and two fingers if they hear a possibility (e.g., `might pursue`). Next, the teacher dictates sentences with advice (e.g., "My counselor said, 'You should get a part-time job.'"). Students listen carefully to identify the advice and then, in pairs, transform it into `reported speech` (e.g., "My counselor `said that I should` get a part-time job."). The teacher explicitly highlights and practices the pronunciation of words with the /iː/ sound (e.g., "achieve," "believe," "volunteer," "engineering," "leadership") and stress patterns in multisyllabic words from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Advice):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A states a `future goal` (e.g., "I want to `achieve` a `career` in `medicine`."). Student B `listens` and `recommends` a `skill` or an `opportunity` using a modal (e.g., "You `might consider` doing `community service` at a hospital to gain `experience`."). Then they swap roles. They listen carefully to each other's suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Plans and Time Capsule."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion and Summary Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group discusses their `future plans` for the "time capsule." They imagine they are putting an item into the capsule that represents a `goal` they want to `achieve` in ten years. Each student explains their chosen item and associated `future goal`. For example: "I am including a photo of my robotics project because it represents my `goal` to become an `electronic engineer` in the `future`."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">As students share, others in the group `listen` actively and offer `advice` or suggest `opportunities` using modals (e.g., "You `could explore` `internships` in `technology` companies," or "You `might develop` `critical thinking skills` through specific projects").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">One student in each group is assigned the role of `recorder` to note down peers' `goals` and `advice` using `reported speech`. Another student is the `presenter` who, at the end of the activity, summarizes one or two group members' plans and the `advice` given to the whole class (e.g., "`Carlos said that he is interested in studying marine biology` and `Maria recommended that he should volunteer at an aquarium` to gain `experience`."). The focus is on coherent listening, fluent expression, and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion and summary presentation, focusing on listening comprehension and accurate language use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket where they summarize (in 2-3 sentences) one `future plan` and a piece of `advice` given to a peer in their group, specifically using `reported speech` and at least two target vocabulary words introduced in the lesson. This assesses their ability to `listen` and comprehend key information, and then accurately paraphrase it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /iː/ and word stress).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of target vocabulary (e.g., `career`, `goals`, `advice`, `internship`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of `modals` for possibilities and `future continuous`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of `reported speech` to convey `advice` or `plans`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in summarizing a peer's plans/advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and collaborative interaction within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new vocabulary word you learned today about `future plans` or `careers`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting `advice` you heard someone give or receive today?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What `skills` do you think you need to `develop` to `achieve` your `future goals`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did `listening` to your classmates' `plans` help you think about your own `future opportunities`?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening activities successfully engaged students with the theme and scenario. The vocabulary introduction and pronunciation practice were effective, especially for words with the /iː/ sound. Students showed good comprehension of the audio and were able to extract key information. The challenge was ensuring consistent use of `reported speech` in spontaneous production. I need to provide more structured practice for `reported speech` in the next lesson's reading context. Students enjoyed discussing their `future goals` and seemed to understand the connection between current `skills` and `future opportunities`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read an article about `high-demand careers` and necessary `skills`, reinforcing the vocabulary and grammatical structures introduced today. They will `read` to comprehend specific details about `career paths` and `training opportunities`.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>